<div class="qandaset block" id="av_1.0-ch03_s03_s03_s03_qs02_ans">
<span id="Answers_2"></span><div mt-section-origin="Courses/Howard_University/General_Chemistry:_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_1:__Atomic_Structure/Chapter_3:__The_Periodic_Table/Chapter_3.3:_Energetics_of_Ion_Formation" class="mt-section"><span id="Answers_2"></span><h3 id="Answers-17436">Answers</h3>

<ol class="qandadiv">
    <li class="qandaentry" id="av_1.0-ch03_s03_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa01_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para">&nbsp;</p>

    <div class="informalfigure large medium-height"><a class="thumb" title="Graph of number of electrons versus ionization energy." rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/27913/c0111099117a4faa3e791b87caa0f403.jpg?revision=1"><img alt="Graph of number of electrons versus ionization energy." class="internal default" style="width: 440px; height: 347px;" loading="lazy" width="440px" height="347px" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/27913/c0111099117a4faa3e791b87caa0f403.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=440&amp;height=347" /></a></div>

    <p class="para" style="text-align: justify;">The general features of both plots are roughly the same, with a small peak at 12 electrons and an essentially level region from 15&ndash;16 electrons. The slope of the <em class="emphasis">I</em><sub class="subscript">3</sub> plot is about twice as large as the slope of the <em class="emphasis">I</em><sub class="subscript">1</sub> plot, however, because the <em class="emphasis">I</em><sub class="subscript">3</sub> values correspond to removing an electron from an ion with a +2 charge rather than a neutral atom. The greater charge increases the effect of the steady rise in effective nuclear charge across the row.</p>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry" id="av_1.0-ch03_s03_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa02_ans">
    <div class="answer">&nbsp;</div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry" id="av_1.0-ch03_s03_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa03_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para" style="text-align: justify;">Electron configurations: Mg, 1<em class="emphasis">s</em><sup class="superscript">2</sup>2<em class="emphasis">s</em><sup class="superscript">2</sup>2<em class="emphasis">p</em><sup class="superscript">6</sup>3<em class="emphasis">s</em><sup class="superscript">2</sup>; Al, 1<em class="emphasis">s</em><sup class="superscript">2</sup>2<em class="emphasis">s</em><sup class="superscript">2</sup>2<em class="emphasis">p</em><sup class="superscript">6</sup>3<em class="emphasis">s</em><sup class="superscript">2</sup>3<em class="emphasis">p</em><sup class="superscript">1</sup>; Si, 1<em class="emphasis">s</em><sup class="superscript">2</sup>2<em class="emphasis">s</em><sup class="superscript">2</sup>2<em class="emphasis">p</em><sup class="superscript">6</sup>3<em class="emphasis">s</em><sup class="superscript">2</sup>3<em class="emphasis">p</em><sup class="superscript">2</sup>; First ionization energies increase across the row due to a steady increase in effective nuclear charge; thus, Si has the highest first ionization energy. The third ionization energy corresponds to removal of a 3<em class="emphasis">s</em> electron for Al and Si, but for Mg it involves removing a 2<em class="emphasis">p</em> electron from a filled inner shell; consequently, the third ionization energy of Mg is the highest.</p>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry" id="av_1.0-ch03_s03_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa04_ans">
    <div class="answer">&nbsp;</div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry" id="av_1.0-ch03_s03_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa05_ans">
    <div class="answer">&nbsp;</div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry" id="av_1.0-ch03_s03_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa06_ans">
    <div class="answer">&nbsp;</div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry" id="av_1.0-ch03_s03_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa07_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para">&nbsp;</p>

    <ol class="orderedlist">
        <li>Bi &gt; As &gt; N</li>
        <li>F &gt; O &gt;&gt; Ar</li>
        <li>Rb &gt; Cs &gt; Ba</li>
    </ol>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry" id="av_1.0-ch03_s03_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa08_ans">
    <div class="answer">&nbsp;</div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry" id="av_1.0-ch03_s03_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa09_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para">Hg<sup class="superscript">2</sup><sup class="superscript">+</sup> &gt; H<sup class="superscript">+</sup> &gt; O<sup class="superscript">&minus;</sup> &gt; N<sup class="superscript">2&minus;</sup>; Hg<sup class="superscript">2</sup><sup class="superscript">+</sup> has the highest positive charge plus a relatively low energy vacant set of orbitals (the 6<em class="emphasis">p</em> subshell) to accommodate an added electron, giving it the greatest electron affinity; N<sup class="superscript">2&minus;</sup> has a greater negative charge than O<sup class="superscript">&minus;</sup>, so electron&ndash;electron repulsions will cause its electron affinity to be even lower (more negative) than that of O<sup class="superscript">&minus;</sup>.</p>
